  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 623 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nVery moist mid-level and boundary layer airmass remains in place in \nmodest northwest upper flow. A weak wave moving into central Kansas \novernight with a weak low-level jet should continue to spark showers \nand a few thunderstorms into the post-sunrise with some potential \nfor an MCV over far southeast Colorado to keep precipitation going \nwell into the afternoon, as supported by recent HRRR runs. \nPrecipitable water values in the 2 to 2.25-inch range will support \nthe potential for locally heavy rain despite high cloud bases. If \nthe precipitation lingers, temperatures may only reach the 80s again \ntoday, though mid 90-degree readings were not far to the south and \nsouthwest Wednesday. A faster end could see these later temps \nmaterialize in central and adjacent north-central Kansas, with \ndewpoints in the mid 70s quite possible (augmented by more rain \ntoday) and bring heat indices to near 105. Confidence in the \ncoverage, persistence, and occurrence of this heat is too low for an \nAdvisory. \n\nA stronger wave continues to look to push southeast through the \nnorthern Plains late today into tonight, with associated \nthunderstorms likely to erupt in north-central Nebraska. These \nstorms may retain some strength as the enter the area after midnight \nin decent effective shear and MUCAPE around 1000 J/kg for a small \ndownburst wind risk this far southeast. The cold front and upper \ntrough axis move through by mid-afternoon Friday but an isolated \nstorm or two could form before it moves out. Temperatures should be \nsomewhat cooler but heat indices around 100 look possible in east-\ncentral Kansas. \n\nA notably-drier and cooler airmass works in for the weekend on \nmeridional upper flow with some modification into the mid-week.",
